WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.669
-Our next guests are Emmy-
nominated writers, creators,

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.538
and stars of shows
such as "Sherman's Showcase"

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.974
and "South Side," which is
now in its third season.

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:10.176
New episodes stream
Thursdays on HBO Max.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.277
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.879
-I'm so happy
you guys are here.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:15.014
This is perfect timing.
It's perfect.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:18.017
Waylon and I were just
discussing the urgent need

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.953
for more Black-owned businesses
right here on the South Side.

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:22.755
-Mm-hmm.
This ain't the South Side.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.091
-And this ain't a Black-owned
business unless, you know,

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.659
he ain't just light-skinned,
is he?

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.327
-No, no, no.
That is a white man. Yes.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:29.796
-What is this?
1 South Michigan?

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.698
So technically
this is the South Side.

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:33.966
-No.
-I mean, you call it that,

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:35.234
and I'm behind that.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:36.869
-No. It's not a thing.
I'm not behind it.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.671
-22nd is usually what
people think is South Side.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.706
-I believe they call this
the Wild Ones?

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:42.208
-We're kind of downtowny-ish.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.443
-Mm-hmm.
-But it's cool.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.345
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:48.614
Bashir Salahuddin
and Diallo Riddle, everyone!

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:50.450
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:57.924 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:05.398 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:08.267
Welcome back, gentlemen!
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.336
I'm so happy
"South Side" is back.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.138
-Yeah, yeah!
-This is a show

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.407
filmed in your hometown.
-South Side of Chicago.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.576
Let's go, Chi-Town!
-There we go.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.811
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you, cousin.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:22.449
-Bashir, I am well aware
that you have a very large cast,

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.650
a lot of speaking parts
in the show.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.218
-Yes.
-A lot of friends and family

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:28.955
probably want
a piece of this apple --

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.523
want a bite of the apple.
-Yeah.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.725
You know, it's nice when I'm
on set and I look around, I go,

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.628
"I'm related to that guy.
I'm married to this woman.

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.597
This dude owes me money."
You know, it's nice.

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.166
-So now you're getting him paid.
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:01:41.166 --> 00:01:43.736
It takes care of itself.
-And I will say, you know,

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.438
knowing it takes place
in Chicago,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.740
knowing how many of these
are local hires

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.975
and how many are
non-professional hires,

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:51.311
people are deeply funny.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:52.912
I don't feel like
you have any cast members

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.347
who don't know
how to deliver a line.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.016
-We always say you could count
on one hand the number of people

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.018
we actually fly in
to be on the show.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.286
-True, true.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.554
-Like, everybody in Chicago
has at some point --

00:02:02.554 --> 00:02:04.557
It's becoming like the Chicago
version of "Law &amp; Order."

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.726
Like, every actor gets a chance
to be on the show.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.228
-We walk down the street
and go,

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:11.330
"Have you been on the show yet?"
-"'Cause you're kind of funny.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.265
You're funny-ish."

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:15.435
Sometimes when we're filming
out on location,

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:16.936
people will come up and be like,

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:19.238
"Hey, man, this is a good scene,
but you know what it's missing?

00:02:19.238 --> 00:02:22.809
It's missing me!"
You know, like...

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.278
"Alright, Uncle."
-Alright.

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:28.414
-You -- I read --
Diallo, I've read you say

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.116
that you want to make this

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.217
like Springfield is
to "The Simpsons,"

00:02:32.217 --> 00:02:33.553
like that big a world.

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:35.388
-Well, listen.
Bashir and I met in college,

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.956
and one of the things
we bonded over

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.624
was our love of "The Simpsons."
-Hm.

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:40.460
-And, you know,
we called it "South Side"

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.296
and not any particular character
name because we really wanted

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:45.632
the community
of the South Side of Chicago

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.267
to be sort of
the star of the show.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.468
-That's right. We have folks

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:50.569
from all over Chicago
who are in the show.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:52.672
In fact, last month,
I was at my mom's house.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:55.208
You know, like a lot of adults,
I was craving a sno-cone.

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:56.209
-Yeah.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.377
[ Laughter ]
-Right?

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.178
-As one does.
-As adults do. Grown-ups.

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:01.047
And then I was like,
"I want a sno-cone."

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.383
She's like, "Oh, there's a dude
on 80th and Vincennes

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:04.850
that sells sno-cones.
They're so good."

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.452
I went out.
The guy was so funny.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.353
He's in the trailer
for season three.

00:03:08.353 --> 00:03:10.890
We just put him on the show.
Yeah, I don't even know

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.558
if he knows that
we were making a TV show.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:15.127
He just was being funny,
and then the cameras came in.

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:16.896
But I'm so happy he did it.
He was so good.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:19.432
-Yeah.
You got a new fan of the show...

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.434
-Yeah.
-...this year, Bashir.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:25.171
So, Bashir, you were in
"Top Gun: Maverick" this year.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.939
-Yeah. I was.
-Congratulations.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:28.107
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.710
-Yeah. You guys saw that?
A little indie film.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.112
-A little indie film.
The same viewership as --

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.647
-Probably, yeah. Same folks.

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.549
-But Tom Cruise --
How does it happen

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:39.118
that you get Tom Cruise
to watch "South Side"?

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.154
-He shot a lot of movies
in Chicago.

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.722
"Color of Money" is the one
I can think of now.

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.224
We were talking about it,

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:47.160
and I was telling him that
I had a TV show set in Chicago.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.594
He's like, "Let me see it."

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.062
So we sent it to him,
and I remember one day

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:52.431
I was in the office
and I was screwing around,

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.134
probably on some websites
that I shouldn't have been on.

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.603
WorldStarHipHop. Sorry.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.038
I was like,
"Nobody with my degree

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.307
should be on this website,"
but I was on it.

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.609
And then I got a phone call,
and I was like, "What is -- "

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.444
And somebody called me.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:07.212
They said, "Hey, Bashir,
I love the show."

00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:09.181
And I was like, "Tom!"

00:04:09.181 --> 00:04:11.250
But he called me,
and it was so much fun,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.387
and I had the great privilege
of walking into the writer room,

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.322
being, "Hey, guys.
Somebody wants to talk to you.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.590
And it's Tom Cruise."
-Wow.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.658
-And, by the way,
they didn't believe it at first.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:21.960
They were like, "That's Tom
Cruise on the line? Alright.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.963
Get Indiana Jones to phone in."

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.331
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:27.533
-"Yeah, Bashir's finally
lost it. It only took..."

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:30.203
-Were you in the room when the
call came in from Tom Cruise?

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.204
-No, I was actually
in the room next door,

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.673
but I noticed right away, like,

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.875
there was a weird sort of,
like, crackling speaker phone...

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:39.778
I was like, "What is Bashir
doing over there?"

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:41.814
And I go over, and I listen
for a couple minutes.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.116
I'm like, "Ah, this is that day

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:46.919
when Tom Cruise
calls the writer room."

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.855
-You guys want to talk
to Maverick. That's right.

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:51.858
-You guys, one of
the centers of the show --

00:04:51.858 --> 00:04:53.793
And I should say
it's all walks of life.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.027
You have cops.

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:56.863
You have people who are
in trouble with the cops.

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:58.297
-Yeah.
-And you have the center of it

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:01.733
is this store called Rent-T-Own.
"Rentione." Excuse me.

00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:03.469
-Unclear how to pronounce that.

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.437
It's got hyphens
in weird places.

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:07.606
Is it Rent Town?
-Two T's back-to-back.

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:09.842
It's one of those things.
We just didn't give a damn.

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:12.077
We just said, "Spell it
the worst way possible."

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:13.712
-And, now,
why did you want to make

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:16.181
this sort of business
the center of this world?

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:17.817
-Well, this is
a terrible business.

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:19.151
-Yeah.
-It's a predatory --

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:20.652
No, really.
If you guys know --

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:22.387
I don't want to call any names,
Rent-A-Center,

00:05:22.387 --> 00:05:24.357
but let's just say...

00:05:24.357 --> 00:05:26.558
[ Laughter ]
Some people know it.

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:28.861
If you're in urban America,
you know,

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:31.097
sometimes you go
to somebody's house

00:05:31.097 --> 00:05:33.466
and they have TV and the couch,
and everything is rented.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.267
And then if they don't pay
for that stuff,

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:37.770
somebody comes in and
snatches it all back from them.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.405
And we thought,
what a terrible business,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.840
but what a great TV show idea.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.875
-Maybe we can mine this
for some comedy.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.310
-There's something funny here.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.478
-One of the twins
on the show, Quincy...

00:05:46.478 --> 00:05:48.715
-Shout-out, Quincy.
-...who plays the manager.

00:05:48.715 --> 00:05:50.750
He used to work
at a Rent-A-Center,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.317
and he used to regale Bashir

00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:53.953
with these stories
about replevins.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.487
It's their form of repossession.

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:58.991
And we did think that, like,
out of this tragic sadness,

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:00.326
there might be some --

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:01.827
there might be
some solid comedy gold.

00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:02.928
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:04.397
-It is --
I should note one of the things

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:06.599
that I like about the show
is no one who works there

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:08.100
thinks it's a nice place
to work, either.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.336
-We definitely
don't sell the idea

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.004
that it's a good business.
-Yeah.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:13.739
-This is a terrible,
terrible place

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:15.374
that you don't want
to be a part of.

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:16.876
But the funny thing is,
is, like,

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:18.310
our folks were a part of it,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.145
and we think that's something
people can relate to.

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.814
A lot of folks out there have,
you know, jobs

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.215
they don't look forward
to going to.

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:25.284
How do you muddle through
those days and still find humor?

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:26.952
And I think that is what makes
our show work,

00:06:26.952 --> 00:06:29.822
is that there's people who have
kind of tough circumstances,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:31.724
but yet they still find
great ways to laugh.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.492
-It is a very deeply funny show.

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:37.096
You shot at the real
Lollapalooza in Chicago.

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:38.431
-Yes. Shout-out Lollapalooza.

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:41.434
-And, now, how is that
when you're shooting

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:43.503
at an ongoing, active
music festival?

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:45.805
-There were a lot of people
trying to jump

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:47.239
in front of the cameras.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:48.607
-Okay.
-There was a lot of that.

00:06:48.607 --> 00:06:50.209
You know, but we had
such a great time.

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:51.710
And, like, shout-out
to Lollapalooza

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.179
for letting us film
at their festival

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:54.814
because they didn't
have to say yes to us.

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:56.348
You know what I mean?
Like, they --

00:06:56.348 --> 00:06:58.885
They allowed us to use
all their production values.

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:01.220
And we had rapper Cordae.
Shout-out to Cordae.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.021
-Shout-out Cordae.
-On the main stage.

00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:05.924
So we got to go up on the
main stage and shoot up there.

00:07:05.924 --> 00:07:08.627
And there were robots. I don't
know if that's a spoiler.

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:10.729
I don't want
to do a spoiler-y interview.

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:12.698
I don't want to do
back-to-back
spoiler-y interviews.

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:14.066
-We've already spoiled
"White Lotus."

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:15.901
-Here come all the "South Side"
season-three spoilers

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:18.971
that you've been avoiding!
[ Laughter ]

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:21.774
-That you weren't even aware
you were avoiding.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:24.176
-You guys do not
just have the one show.

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:27.012
I also want to give a shout-out
to "Sherman's Showcase."

00:07:27.012 --> 00:07:28.347
-Let's go.
-Thank you.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.616
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:32.718
-This is another

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.320
really beautiful piece
of world-building.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.191
This is sort of a fictional show
inspired by "Soul Train"...

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:41.861
-Yes.
-...and inspired by so many

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:44.463
different musical acts
of a certain era.

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:47.400
-Listen, we always said
that if a show like "30 Rock"

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.870
took place at "SNL," our show
takes place at "Soul Train."

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.571
And we always thought that,
like --

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:54.907
You know, 'cause
we came up working at "Fallon."

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.309
We were always like --
how do we take these songs

00:07:57.309 --> 00:07:59.712
that aren't really "Fallon"
or NBC-appropriate --

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.113
where do we give them a home?

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:02.481
And the home ended up
being "Sherman's Showcase."

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:04.283
-That's right.
We can do all the music

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.218
that NBC was like,
"I don't think so."

00:08:06.218 --> 00:08:07.653
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:10.089
-"Drop It Low for Jesus"?
Not today.

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:11.590
-I don't think so.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.193
-And you're now three --
You have three actual albums

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:16.662
that have come out
of your television show.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:20.065
-Yes. Quality albums out on
Mad Decent, but, you know --

00:08:20.065 --> 00:08:21.934
-I was going to say. We always
loved The Lonely Island.

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.035
We love those guys.
-Yeah.

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.303
-And we love
that their music --

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:26.105
I remember when --
you know, Diallo was a deejay.

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.207
We used to live in New York.
He would be deejaying parties.

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:30.242
He would put on things like --

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:31.844
-I remember I put on
"Lazy Sunday."

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.579
-And it killed.
-The party kept going.

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:35.147
-Yes.
-And we were always like --

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:37.250
And today, by the way,
turtlenecks and chains.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:38.951
So I don't know
what I was thinking.

00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:40.552
-You guys can't see
the audience,

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:41.920
but everybody's in turtlenecks.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.922
-Yes. Turn the camera that way.

00:08:43.922 --> 00:08:45.724
-We cannot afford
an audience camera,

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:47.493
but I assure you that's right.

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:49.461
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.461 --> 00:08:51.696
-But we wanted to make songs
that you could appreciate

00:08:51.696 --> 00:08:54.132
whether you're listening to them
at home or in the club,

00:08:54.132 --> 00:08:55.968
and that means a lot.
Like, this season,

